348
02:07:08,659 --> 02:07:16,659
From the tallest towers to the largest tunnels, some of the world's largest civil engineering wonders are just being built.

349
02:07:16,659 --> 02:07:26,659
The capabilities of civil engineers and architects are only growing as engineering software and scientific research advance their fields.

350
02:07:26,659 --> 02:07:35,659
While you may look back on projects and think that the Hoover Dam or the Burj Khalifa is the best civil engineering will ever get,

351
02:07:35,659 --> 02:07:39,659
Let's take a look at a few projects that might be even more impressive.

352
02:07:39,659 --> 02:07:54,659
Las Vegas Convention Center Tunnel. Above and underneath the well-trodden streets of Sin City is the development of a $52.5 million tram network for the Las Vegas Convention Center.

353
02:07:54,659 --> 02:07:59,659
While work initially began above ground on this high profile transportation system,

354
02:07:59,659 --> 02:08:06,659
The below ground tunneling work marks the first commercial project for Elon Musk's boring company.

355
02:08:06,659 --> 02:08:20,659
The tunnel will span 1.5 miles under Las Vegas and will be capable of moving 4,400 passengers per hour, turning a 15 minute walk into a two minute ride.

356
02:08:20,659 --> 02:08:27,659
While this may not seem all that impressive, it's a major milestone in underground engineering.

357
02:08:27,659 --> 02:08:40,659
Tunnel digging may be centuries old, but commercializing the process for transport on a large scale could well expand the use of transportation tunnels to many other metropolises.

358
02:08:40,659 --> 02:08:54,659
Musk and the boring company have already broken through one part of the tunnel, and they expect to have the project open to the public in January 2021, just in time for the CES, if it happens.

359
02:08:54,659 --> 02:08:57,659
Jeddah Tower, Saudi Arabia.

360
02:08:57,659 --> 02:09:04,659
While you've probably heard of the Burj Khalifa, you might not have heard the name Jeddah Tower yet.

361
02:09:04,659 --> 02:09:16,659
Construction on the tower began in 2013, and when it's completed in 2021, it's set to take the new crown as the world's tallest skyscraper.

362
02:09:16,659 --> 02:09:23,659
However, the construction process has been plagued with labor and staffing issues, causing it to make very slow progress.

363
02:09:23,659 --> 02:09:39,659
Also known as the Kingdom Tower, the building will reach a minimum of .62 miles into the air, and will be the centerpiece of the Jeddah Economic City, which is a $20 billion project stretched over two square miles.

364
02:09:39,659 --> 02:09:42,659
Hong Kong Zhuhai-Makai Bridge.

365
02:09:42,659 --> 02:09:52,659
Now that we've examined two impressive projects that are planned to be completed soon, let's take a look at a civil engineering wonder that was recently finished.

366
02:09:52,659 --> 02:10:04,659
Construction on the Hong Kong Zhuhai-Makai Bridge began all the way back in 2009, and was completed in February of 2018.

367
02:10:04,659 --> 02:10:09,659
This civil engineering mega project isn't just your normal bridge, though.

368
02:10:09,659 --> 02:10:16,659
It's the longest sea crossing and the longest open sea fixed link in the entire world.

369
02:10:16,659 --> 02:10:29,659
Designed to last 120 years, the bridge spans the Ling Ding and Zhuhiao channels, connecting Hong Kong, Macau, and Zhuhai, three major cities on the Pearl River Delta.

370
02:10:29,659 --> 02:10:39,659
The project involved constructing a series of interconnected bridges, artificial islands, and tunnels, and cost around $18.8 billion.

371
02:10:39,659 --> 02:10:44,659
It stretches of blistering 34 miles across the open ocean.

372
02:10:44,659 --> 02:10:55,659
The main section includes a 14.2 mile viaduct and a 4.2 mile undersea tunnel that runs between two artificial islands.

373
02:10:55,659 --> 02:10:58,659
High Speed Railway, California.

374
02:10:58,659 --> 02:11:28,659
California.

375
02:10:59,347 --> 02:11:08,707
While the United States has long had a network of railroads running from east to west, what it doesn't have is particularly fast trains.

376
02:11:08,707 --> 02:11:21,108
While there are some high-speed rail networks operated at speeds of around 100 miles per hour, that's no match for the bullet trains of Japan, which regularly run at 200 miles per hour.

377
02:11:21,108 --> 02:11:28,228
However, all of that is starting to change. California is working on constructing a high-speed train network

378
02:11:28,228 --> 02:11:42,868
that could completely change the transportation landscape of the state. Construction on the project began back in 2015, but it won't be completed until 2029, assuming there aren't any delays.

379
02:11:42,868 --> 02:11:52,787
It will cover a distance of 280 miles and provide a connection between the state's two population centers in just two hours and 40 minutes.

380
02:11:52,787 --> 02:12:04,547
Work is continuing on a 171 mile core segment of the track, but due to massive budget overruns, the rest of the track's completion has been thrown into limbo.

381
02:12:04,547 --> 02:12:11,188
If completed, this will be a big leap forward for U.S. transportation infrastructure projects.

382
02:12:11,188 --> 02:12:20,148
The future of civil engineering projects is looking bright. We're building taller towers, bigger, more usable tunnels,

383
02:12:20,148 --> 02:12:23,908
and longer bridges, and more impressive structures.

384
02:12:23,908 --> 02:12:31,908
It seems that nothing can overcome the desire to build things bigger and better than what's been done before.

